==============================================================================
0) INTUITION FIRST — WHAT PROBLEM ARE WE EVEN SOLVING?
==============================================================================

WHY THIS CONCEPT EXISTS
In distributed systems, one business action often touches multiple services/databases.

Example:
- Amazon order placement
- Payment service deducts money
- Inventory service reserves stock
- Order service creates order row
- Notification service sends confirmation

Now imagine:
- Payment succeeds
- Inventory update fails

Without a distributed transaction:
- user loses money
- no order created
- stock inconsistent
- support nightmare

This is exactly WHY 2PC exists:
It guarantees ATOMICITY across multiple distributed nodes.

Atomicity = ALL succeed OR ALL rollback.

This is the distributed version of ACID transaction atomicity.

REAL LIFE ANALOGY
Think of buying a movie ticket:
- Bank says “money reserved”
- Theater says “seat reserved”
- Only when both confirm → final booking
- If any side says no → both undo

This “first ask everyone if they are ready, then finalize” is 2PC.

CORE IDEA
2PC splits commit into 2 phases:
1) Prepare / Voting phase
2) Commit / Decision phase

This prevents partial commit across multiple machines.

Golden line:
2PC gives atomic commit across distributed participants.

==============================================================================
1) WHY NORMAL LOCAL TRANSACTION IS NOT ENOUGH
==============================================================================

WHY THIS EXISTS
A single DB transaction works only inside one database engine.

But modern systems are:
- microservices
- multiple databases
- geo-distributed
- polyglot persistence
- separate ownership boundaries

Examples:
- Uber trip completion:
  - trip DB
  - payment DB
  - driver payout ledger
- WhatsApp premium business payment:
  - wallet DB
  - invoice DB
  - analytics DB
- Netflix billing:
  - subscription DB
  - payment processor
  - invoice storage

A local DB transaction cannot span all these safely.

Hence:
Distributed transaction protocol needed → 2PC.

==============================================================================
2) CORE 2PC FLOW — THE BIG PICTURE
==============================================================================

PARTICIPANTS
1) Coordinator / Transaction Manager
   - drives protocol
   - collects votes
   - makes final decision

2) Participants / Resource Managers
   - actual databases/services
   - execute local transaction
   - vote yes/no

WHY THIS ARCHITECTURE
We need one authority to avoid inconsistent decisions.

Problem solved:
- prevents some nodes committing while others abort

Tradeoff:
- coordinator becomes SPOF risk
- blocking protocol

When NOT ideal:
- internet-scale microservices
- long-running workflows
- user-facing low latency paths
- highly partitioned systems

==============================================================================
3) PHASE 1 — PREPARE / VOTING PHASE
==============================================================================

WHY THIS PHASE EXISTS
Before committing globally, every participant must promise:
“I can commit this safely later.”

This avoids partial success.

FLOW
Step 1:
Coordinator sends PREPARE to all participants.

Step 2:
Each participant:
- executes transaction locally
- writes undo/redo log
- locks required rows/resources
- ensures durability on disk
- replies:
  - YES (ready)
  - NO (cannot commit)

WHY FORCE LOGGING HERE
Crash recovery.

If participant crashes after voting YES,
it must remember:
“I promised commit.”

This is critical for recovery.

REAL EXAMPLE — PAYMENT + INVENTORY
User buys iPhone on Amazon:
- payment DB reserves ₹80,000
- inventory DB reserves 1 item
- order DB prepares row insertion

Each says YES only after durable local prepare.

Latency tradeoff:
prepare requires fsync → disk I/O expensive.

==============================================================================
4) PHASE 2 — COMMIT / ABORT PHASE
==============================================================================

WHY THIS PHASE EXISTS
Once everyone says YES, system can safely make one final decision.

FLOW
If ALL YES:
- coordinator logs COMMIT
- sends COMMIT to all

If ANY NO:
- coordinator logs ABORT
- sends ROLLBACK to all

Participants then:
- commit local tx
OR
- rollback local tx

Atomicity guarantee:
either everyone commits or everyone aborts.

IMPORTANT INTERVIEW LINE
Atomicity comes from:
- unanimous voting
- durable coordinator decision log
- deterministic final action

